  try to identify as people have the virus  and recover from it a lot of people  actually don't even know or they have  very mild symptoms and so they've  they've they're actually immunized  against the virus but and it's a big but  it is not yet 100 percent certain people  with antibodies will be immune or how  long immunity would last the virus could  mutate so the optimism is an educated  guess right now  dr. Craig Jenny is an epidemiologist at  the University of Calgary  he says the antibody test could help in  the development of a vaccine but the  reality is a vaccine could still be a  year to 18 months or longer away until  then we could be confined to our bubbles  physical distancing remains the most  effective way to slow the spread it's  possible the virus could in several  months be contained enough to start  relaxing some restrictions on our lives  for now the restaurants theaters malls  and countless businesses will stay dark  as we wait for the doctors researchers  scientists and public health experts to  lead us back into the light with me now  is dr. Taz Lehman mg she's director of  medical innovation at Humber River  Hospital in Toronto dr. NIMH G thank you  so much for all you do and for joining  us you've been treating patients with  kovat 19 you're on the front lines of  this we've heard Canadian hospitals are  still coping have not been overwhelmed  what's it been like for you I think  that's fair to say we we certainly are  coping I think we've created a lot of  capacity in the system that's that's  helped us certainly in terms of dealing  with the traffic now coming into the  system and in addition I I think  certainly we can see when we're looking  at our numbers and doing our modeling  the efforts that we have in the  community are really helping and that's  in terms of folks social distancing and  not being out and about is making a big  difference and of course we say that not  to suggest that it's made a difference  it continues to make a 